data.content:
# EF Core 10 Breaking Changes for Your Code

## 1. `SetProperty` — value argument must now be a lambda expression

In EF Core 10, the overload accepting a raw value/constant was removed. The value must be an `Expression<Func<TSource, TProperty>>` lambda, even in manually-built expression trees.

**Fix:** Wrap `Expression.Constant("foo")` in a lambda:

```csharp
var blogParameter = Expression.Parameter(typeof(Blog), "b");
var valueParameter = Expression.Parameter(typeof(Blog), "b2");

setters = Expression.Lambda<Func<SetPropertyCalls<Blog>, SetPropertyCalls<Blog>>>(
    Expression.Call(
        instance: setters.Body,
        methodName: nameof(SetPropertyCalls<Blog>.SetProperty),
        typeArguments: new[] { typeof(string) },
        arguments: new Expression[]
        {
            Expression.Lambda<Func<Blog, string>>(
                Expression.Property(blogParameter, nameof(Blog.Name)), blogParameter),
            // NEW: value must be a lambda, not a bare constant
            Expression.Lambda<Func<Blog, string>>(
                Expression.Constant("foo"), valueParameter)
        }),
    setters.Parameters);
```

Also update the initial literal setter:
```csharp
Expression<Func<SetPropertyCalls<Blog>, SetPropertyCalls<Blog>>> setters =
    s => s.SetProperty(b => b.Views, b => 8);  // value is now a lambda
```

---

## 2. Complex types — default column naming now prefixes with the navigation name

EF Core 10 disambiguates same-named properties across complex types by including the navigation path in the column name (e.g., `ShippingAddress_Street` / `BillingAddress_Street`). Existing databases will fail on migration unless you pin the old names.

**Fix:** Explicitly configure column names to match your existing schema:

```csharp
modelBuilder.Entity<Customer>(b =>
{
    b.ComplexProperty(c => c.ShippingAddress, ab =>
    {
        ab.Property(a => a.Street).HasColumnName("Street");
        // ... other properties
    });
    b.ComplexProperty(c => c.BillingAddress, ab =>
    {
        ab.Property(a => a.Street).HasColumnName("BillingStreet");
        // ... match whatever your DB already has
    });
});

// For nested: Order.Payment.BillingAddress.City
modelBuilder.Entity<Order>(b =>
{
    b.ComplexProperty(o => o.Payment, pb =>
    {
        pb.ComplexProperty(p => p.BillingAddress, ab =>
        {
            ab.Property(a => a.City).HasColumnName("Payment_BillingAddress_City");
        });
    });
});
```

---

## 3. `IRelationalCommandDiagnosticsLogger` — new `CommandSource` parameter

EF Core 10 added a `CommandSource` parameter to the diagnostics methods.

**Fix:**

```csharp
public class MyLogger : IRelationalCommandDiagnosticsLogger
{
    public InterceptionResult<DbDataReader> CommandReaderExecuting(
        IRelationalConnection connection,
        DbCommand command,
        DbContext context,
        Guid commandId,
        Guid connectionId,
        DateTimeOffset startTime,
        CommandSource commandSource)   // <-- new required parameter
    { /* logging */ }
}
```

---

**Summary of action items:**

| Area | Change |
|------|--------|
| `SetProperty` expression trees | Wrap value in `Expression.Lambda<Func<T,TProp>>(...)` |
| Complex types | Pin `HasColumnName()` to preserve existing column names |
| Diagnostics logger | Add `CommandSource commandSource` to all interface methods |
